to \code{prediction_time} and on being event-free at \code{prediction_time}.

This is the single, unified entry point for biomarker-value prediction:
\itemize{
\item If \code{bio_i} names \strong{exactly one} biomarker, this
predicts just that biomarker and returns a single
\code{"dynamicPredictionBio.BJM"} object.
\item If \code{bio_i} names \strong{more than one} biomarker, or is left
at its default \code{NULL} (meaning every biomarker in
\code{long_fit_all}), this instead predicts every requested biomarker
and returns a named list of \code{"dynamicPredictionBio.BJM"} objects
(classed \code{"dynamicPredictionBioAll.BJM"}), computing the
bio_i-\strong{independent} part of the pipeline (restricting to at-risk
patients, the survival-side integration grid, and the denominator
conditional density) only \strong{once} and reusing it across every
biomarker, instead of recomputing it once per biomarker. This matters
most under the Gaussian-copula path (see
\code{\link{longitudinalSubCopula}}), where that denominator involves
\code{mvtnorm::pmvnorm()} Monte-Carlo evaluations that are otherwise
the dominant cost of a multi-biomarker prediction run.
}
See Value for the exact return shape in each case.

\code{bio_i} may refer to either \strong{continuous} or \strong{ordinal}
biomarkers (see \code{\link{longitudinalSubCopula}}), and a single call
may mix both. For an ordinal biomarker, \code{Y_all}/\code{Y_predict} hold
integer \emph{category codes} (\code{1:K}, in the fitted factor's
\code{levels()}/threshold order) rather than a numeric grid, with
\code{Y_all} additionally carrying a \code{"category_labels"} attribute
giving the matching level-label strings; \code{bandcount3} is ignored for
that biomarker, since its candidate grid is fixed at its category count
(see Details).

The prediction is conditional on the longitudinal history observed up to
\code{prediction_time}: rows of \code{data_predict_all} whose
\code{time_variable} is later than \code{prediction_time} are dropped,
with a warning, before predicting.

\code{bandcount2} (controlling the shared survival-integration grid) and
\code{bandcount3} (controlling each biomarker's own candidate-value grid)
are auto-tuned separately when left at their default \code{"auto"}, via
the doubling-until-stable check described in Details. When \code{bio_i}
names more than one biomarker, \code{bandcount2} is resolved \strong{once}
using a single representative biomarker (the first one in \code{bio_i}),
and \code{bandcount3} is then resolved independently for \strong{every}
requested biomarker (their candidate-value grids need not converge at the
same resolution) -- except for any \strong{ordinal} biomarker, for which
\code{bandcount3} tuning is always skipped (see above) and
\code{"bandcount3"} is recorded as \code{NA} for that biomarker.
